| **i124** | **Emulate the full Trinity boot path so the netboot *boot wrappers* run end-to-end in the harness (close the emulate-before-hardware gap)** (Pete, 2026-06-18 — after `client_main` shipped to hardware un-emulated and wasted a debugging session). **Concrete first target to reproduce:** the bootable client (record 13), even after the i119/i125 section-C fix, transmits **nothing** on the wire (hardware-confirmed 2026-06-18 via a wired-Pi raw-socket capture — only the router's ARP appears, *no* SAM frame under any MAC) → it hangs in `client_main` **before** `client_first`. The init path (EEPROM read → `drv_init`) is **byte-identical to the proven `smoke_main`**, and `drv_init`/`client_first` pass individually in the harness — so the bug is either a `client_main` logic detail or a **hardware-specific interaction** (the client's *proactive* ENC TX vs smoke's *reactive* sends, or EEPROM timing). Build this emulation to reproduce/localize it; **if `client_main` runs clean in emulation (reaches `client_first` + emits the ARP), the bug is hardware-specific** → fall back to minimal targeted instrumentation (border-colour markers through `client_main`/`drv_init`/`client_first`), NOT more blind SD-shuffling. The Go/koron-z80 harness already stubs the **LAN** (ENC28J60, `enc28j60.go`) and exercises the phase machines + `drv_init`, but the **boot entry points** (`client_main`/`smoke_main`/`server_main`) are compiled out (`ifndef NETBOOT_HOSTTEST`) because the harness has no **EEPROM config read** (`eeprom.asm` excluded) or **SD/B-DOS storage** (RST-8 HSAVE/HLOAD/HRECORD excluded). Stub those two (the "Trinity Network" config chunk + the record store) so the *whole* boot wrapper runs in emulation — catching logic bugs + enabling **autonomous iteration with no SD-card shuffling**. **Also model the SAM memory map (Pete, 2026-06-18):** mark **ROM1 read-only at `&C000` at boot** + LMPR/HMPR paging in the harness, so loading an **over-size or mis-paged** boot image **reproduces the real crash in emulation** (the flat all-RAM harness has no ROM1 there, so the program-vs-ROM conflict is invisible — which is exactly why this bug slipped through). **Reuse what exists:** the assembler harness `tools/z80-test-harness-go/harness.go` ALREADY models LMPR/HMPR paging (OUT-driven) — reuse that machinery; it just maps section D to **RAM page 3** (the assembler pages RAM there), so the *specific* missing piece is **ROM1-read-only-at-`&C000`-at-boot** (which neither the assembler nor the flat netboot harness models — which is exactly why this slipped through). Lightweight first cut: mark `&C000+` non-writable at boot (catches the over-size class, no ROM contents needed); fuller version loads the SAM ROM so the boot wrappers' ROM calls (`RST 16` print, `SETSTRM`, `CLSBL`) run too. (i125's fit-check is the cheap immediate guard for the same class; this gives the faithful in-emulator catch + *logic* coverage + removes the manual hardware loop.) Confirm the EEPROM + SD stubs are the last missing Trinity pieces. | OPEN — 🔨 boot-path emulation landed (PR #423): EEPROM modeled (`eeprom.go` from the real `eeprom.asm`) + ROM1-read-only-at-`&C000` (`LoadBoot`/`RunBoot`); `smoke_main` (control, reply == Go authority) + `client_main` run end-to-end in the harness. **i82 hang localized:** `client_main`'s logic is sound (reaches `client_first`, emits the ARP), so the fault is the real-silicon layer the model idealizes — root cause = **proactive ARP TX before PHY link-up + no ARP retransmit** (→ i127); smoke escapes it by being reactive. **Remaining:** model PHY link-up (LSTAT + drop-TX-while-down) to *reproduce* the drop in emulation; SD/RST-8 stub for the full client fetch; fold server/serve/http wrappers + delete the carve-outs (i126). | `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/{eeprom,harness,enc28j60,netboot_boot_test}.go`; `src/netboot/eeprom.asm`, `src/netboot/bdos_seam.asm`; i119, i125, i126, i127, i93 |
| **i126** | **One faithful SAM emulation layer, used by every test — fix the fragmented/inadequate emulation** (Pete, 2026-06-18; structural enforcement of CLAUDE.md discipline **rule 7**). Root cause of the i82 hardware-debug detour: **three partial emulators** with gaps the netboot boot path fell straight through — the **flat 64 KB netboot harness** (`tools/netboot-oracle/z80`: no paging/ROM/EEPROM), the **full assembler harness** (`tools/z80-test-harness-go/harness.go`: has LMPR/HMPR paging but maps section D to **RAM**, never models **ROM1-at-`&C000`-at-boot**), and **SimCoupé** (faithful SAM but **no Trinity** ENC/EEPROM/SD). So `client_main` was `NETBOOT_HOSTTEST`-excluded and tested only on hardware. **Deliverable:** consolidate to **one** emulation layer that (a) distinguishes **ROM vs RAM** + models **ROM1 read-only at `&C000` at boot** (cheap first cut: mark `&C000+` non-writable pre-paging — the exact gap that hid the over-size crash), (b) carries the **Trinity seams** (ENC28J60 — already stubbed — + the EEPROM config chunk + the SD/B-DOS record store), (c) is **used by every test** (fold the flat harness into a *mode* of the one layer, not a parallel harness), (d) runs the netboot **boot wrappers** end-to-end so the `NETBOOT_HOSTTEST` carve-outs are **deleted, not maintained**. Reuse harness.go's existing paging. Subsumes i124 (its EEPROM/ROM stub is the netboot slice of this). | OPEN — 🔨 architecture (Pete-flagged, high-level) | `tools/z80-test-harness-go/harness.go` (paging to reuse); `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/` (flat harness to fold in); `src/netboot/*` (`NETBOOT_HOSTTEST` carve-outs to delete); CLAUDE.md discipline rule 7; `feedback_emulation_first`; i124, i125, i116 |
| **i129** | **trinload stage-one bootstrap disk + bring-up flow (de-risk the disk-shipping top priority; Pete, 2026-06-18).** Build a bootable `trinload.mgt` (Simon Owen's trinload, `~/git/trinload`, BSD-ish — the same source `encdrv.asm`/`eeprom.asm` are vendored from) so a Trinity record can boot it as a proven, *reactive* stage-one bootloader that network-loads a code file into **ephemeral RAM** and runs it — no record write, no SD-card dance. Two uses: (a) load + run our netboot tools (e.g. the TFTP server) over the wire for **dev iteration** without re-flashing a record each time; (b) the ready **fallback transport** if our own client/server stalls — trinload loads our code, which then does the fetch-and-persist. NB: trinload runs code in RAM; persisting disk images to records still needs our i119 write-out — trinload removes the SD dance for *getting our code running*, it does not replace the persistence goal. Building the `.mgt` is host-autonomous; **writing it to a Trinity record + booting it is Pete's hardware step** (the SD card). Open Q: whether to vendor `trinload.asm` in-repo (consistent with the existing encdrv/eeprom vendoring) or keep the `.mgt` a local build artifact. | OPEN — 🔨 **`.mgt` built + WRITTEN to Trinity record 128** (2026-06-19, agent over SSH; guard-confirmed empty first; verified `samdos2`+`AUTOtrin.O`). ✅ push→run→return HARDWARE-CONFIRMED 2026-06-19 (code.bin pushed via `trinload3.py` → border cycled → trinload survived); also on rec 3 (identical image). Effectively DONE; vendor-in-repo Q SETTLED by i132 (trinload.asm vendored at `src/netboot/trinload.asm`); move to closed at next sweep. | `~/git/trinload/trinload.asm`; `tools/build-disk/`; i82, i119, i121, i130; the disk-shipping top priority |
| **i130** | **Name every unnamed-but-occupied Trinity record so none gets accidentally wiped (Pete, 2026-06-19).** The card (Atom Lite, 65535 records) holds Pete's whole collection — records 10→817+, file counts 1-80 — but **most occupied records are UNNAMED** (present via `samdisk dir`, absent from `samdisk list`), so a `list`-gap looks free when it isn't (the documented gotcha). Goal: give each occupied-but-unnamed record a label (Pete's spec: `L<rec>`) so `list` shows it. **Mechanism BLOCKER:** `samdisk` 3.8.8 has no name/label subcommand — a record's name comes from the disk *image* written to it, so naming needs the SAM/Atom disk-name storage format pinned down (research the `~/git/samdisk` *current checkout* — older non-head version matching 3.8.8; head is broken-on-mac alpha) then a careful per-record **read-modify-write** (back up each record image first; **test on ONE** + verify files intact + the name shows in `list`; only THEN the Python bulk loop). High-stakes — irreplaceable collection; do NOT bulk-write on an unvalidated mechanism. Empty records found (safe to use, do NOT need naming): 123, 128 (=trinload), 145, 171, 172. | ✅ DONE 2026-06-19 — 805 records named (method validated on rec 128 first, then Python `L<rec>` bulk over 1-850, 0 data failures, all verified). Cosmetic: ~424 show a trailing leftover char (samdisk copy writes only the 10-byte label; harmless — record is named+visible). Move to closed-registry at next sweep | `~/git/samdisk` (source, current checkout); SAMdisk 3.8.8; [[trinity_hardware_first_light]]; i129 |
