| **i137** | **`build-disk`'s netboot ceiling check doesn't distinguish section class (i125 follow-up, found 2026-06-19).** `tools/build-disk/main.go:516` rejects a netboot image only if it loads past the `&10000` ceiling (≤32768 bytes) — a single budget for all. But a *section-C* bootable program (smoke/serve/server/client) must fit `&8000-&BFFF` (≤16384); above that it loads into ROM1 at boot and crashes (the i119 class). So a 16385–32768-byte section-C image passes `build-disk` but would crash on hardware. **Mostly masked today:** the per-program `make netboot-*-boot` fit-check (i125, `netboot-boot-fit-check.sh`, 16384 for section-C / 32768 for the http overlay) fires *first* in the normal `make netboot-*-disk` flow, so an over-section-C binary never reaches `build-disk`. The gap is only reachable by calling `build-disk -netboot <bin>` directly on an over-section-C image. Fix would need `build-disk` to know each image's section class (a `-netboot-section-c` flag threaded through the disk targets) so its last-line-of-defence check matches the make gate. Low priority (masked); tracked so it isn't lost. | OPEN — 🔨 minor robustness (masked by the i125 make gate) | `tools/build-disk/main.go` (the `&10000` netboot check); `tools/netboot-boot-fit-check.sh`; i125, i119 |
| **i139** | **Autonomous-loop monitor: `CONTEXT_SETTLE` 5s too short → the task-done resume nudge is dropped** (Pete, 2026-06-19; follow-up to i136/#428). After `touch task-done`, the monitor stuffs `/context`, waits `CONTEXT_SETTLE`, then stuffs the `RESUME_NUDGE` (the continue-vs-wind-down decision rule). `/context` is a full-TUI redraw; with the settle at 5s the nudge keystrokes land mid-render and are dropped, so no next turn fires and the agent sits idle until the ~1h hang-timeout (observed twice this session — `/context` rendered, no nudge followed). Fix: bump `CONTEXT_SETTLE` default 5→30 (matches the proven `CLEAR_SETTLE`); the comment already called it "the /context analogue of CLEAR_SETTLE". **A running monitor must be restarted to apply** (or relaunch with `ALOOP_CONTEXT_SETTLE=30`). Superseded by i140: the longer sleep did not fix it — the real cause was burst-size, not a render race. | OPEN — 🔨 fix in PR; needs monitor restart to take effect | `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh` (`CONTEXT_SETTLE`); i136 (#428, added the settle); `tools/autonomous-loop/README.md`; i140 |
| **i140** | **monitor.sh task-done nudge not delivered — the second message after `/context` is dropped** (Pete, 2026-06-19, after i139). On TASK_DONE the monitor stuffs `/context` then (after `CONTEXT_SETTLE`) `RESUME_NUDGE`, but the nudge never becomes a turn, so the loop stalls at the checkpoint until the ~1h hang-timeout. i139 bumped `CONTEXT_SETTLE` 5→30 (needs a monitor RESTART) but the symptom persists. Root cause (reproduced in an isolated test screen session, never the live one): a long string stuffed as ONE `screen -X stuff` burst right after `/context`'s full-TUI redraw is dropped wholesale (0/4 delivered) — not a render race a longer sleep can win. Fixed: `stuff` now chunks every payload into small bursts, and the task-done branch uses `nudge_until_turn` (verify-and-retry — polls the screen for the `esc to interrupt` turn marker). Verified end-to-end by running the modified monitor against a fresh isolated session (`nudge delivered (attempt 1, 1s)`, agent took a real turn, twice). | OPEN — 🔨 root-caused + fixed; verified in isolated session | `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh`; `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor-nudge-delivery.md`; `tools/autonomous-loop/README.md`; i139, i97 |
